Effect, dependency, and ref discipline is not restated here. See react-avoid-effects.mdc,
react-effects-dependencies.mdc, and react-refs-dom.mdc.

Project conventions win. Read the neighbouring files first.

## Declaration

✅ export function ChecklistCard({ checklist }: IChecklistCardProps) {}
✅ export const AppHeader = (props: IAppHeaderProps) => <PageHeader {...props} />;
Arrow-const only for a one-expression wrapper. Named exports only. No `React.FC`.

❌ function Card(props: ICardProps) { const { checklist } = props; }
✅ function Card({ checklist }: ICardProps) {}

✅ interface IPromptProps { reason: TNotEnabledReason }
A named props interface even at one prop.

✅ export function XProvider({ children }: React.PropsWithChildren<IXProps>) {}

## Rendering

❌ return isLoading ? <Spinner /> : !isAvailable ? <Prompt /> : <Tree />;
✅ if (isLoading) {
     return <Spinner />;
   }
   if (!isAvailable) {
     return <Prompt reason={reason} />;
   }
   return <Tree />;
A stack of guard returns, then one JSX return. `return null` is normal.

❌ {items.length && <List items={items} />}       // renders a stray "0"
✅ {items.length > 0 && <List items={items} />}
Conditional rendering is `{cond && …}`, with the condition coerced to a real boolean.

✅ <EmptyPrompt data-testid="notEnabledPrompt" />
`data-testid` on the root of every exported component and anything a test asserts.

## State

Server data lives in the query cache. Shared page state lives in context. User-visible filters and
tabs live in the URL. `useState` is for ephemeral local UI only, such as open/closed or hover.

❌ useEffect(() => setBlocksActivity(isLoading || isBlocked), [isLoading, isBlocked]);
✅ const blocksActivity = isLoading || isError || isBlocked;

Drill props freely. Context narrows badly in TypeScript, so reserve it for app-wide concerns
(auth, theme, platform services) where narrowing does not matter.

✅ export function useX() {
     const context = useContext(Ctx);
     if (!context) {
       throw new Error('useX must be used within XProvider');
     }
     return context;
   }
Every context ships a consumer hook that throws when the provider is missing.

No `memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` without a measurement, or a comment naming the referential
stability it protects.
